  from jsonb_array_elements('[{"n":35,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A town''s population P after t years is modelled as P = 12 000 × 1.03^t.\n\n(a) State the population growth rate per year.\n(b) Calculate the population after 10 years, to the nearest whole person.\n(c) Calculate the time for the population to double, to 2 decimal places.\n(d) State one limitation of this model.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A town''s population P after t years is modelled as P = 12 000 × 1.03^t."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the population growth rate per year.\n(b) Calculate the population after 10 years, to the nearest whole person.\n(c) Calculate the time for the population to double, to 2 decimal places.\n(d) State one limitation of this model."}]}]},"e":"The multiplier 1.03 is a 3% annual increase. P(10) ≈ 16 127. Doubling: t = log 2 / log 1.03 ≈ 23.45 years. The model assumes unlimited growth at a constant rate, which no real town sustains.","m":9,"df":"hard","tp":"modelling","st":"modelling","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":35,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"For an exponential model, the base gives the rate and logarithms give the time to reach a target.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the annual growth rate, as a percentage.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Subtract 1 from the multiplier 1.03.","0.03 as a percentage."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":3,"tolerance":0,"unit":"%"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the population after 10 years.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Substitute t = 10.","1.03^10 ≈ 1.3439."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":16127,"tolerance":2,"unit":"people"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the doubling time, in years, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Solve 1.03^t = 2 using logarithms.","t = log 2 ÷ log 1.03."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":23.45,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"years"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State and justify one limitation of the model.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Does a real population grow at a constant rate forever?","What about migration, housing or resources?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["it assumes a constant 3% growth forever, but real populations are limited by housing, resources and migration, so the rate will not stay constant"],"keywords":[["assumes"],["constant"],["growth"],["forever"],["real"],["populations"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for substitution, 1 for 1.03^10, 1 for 16 127. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logs, 1 for 23.45. (d) 2 marks: 1 for a valid limitation, 1 for justification.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for substitution, 1 for 1.03^10, 1 for 16 127. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logs, 1 for 23.45. (d) 2 marks: 1 for a valid limitation, 1 for justification.","response_tools":["graph","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"graph","response_guidance":"Plot the model in the workspace to check the doubling time visually before computing it."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for substitution, 1 for 1.03^10, 1 for 16 127. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the log equation, 1 for the evaluated logs, 1 for 23.45. (d) 2 marks: 1 for a valid limitation, 1 for justification."},{"n":36,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A rectangle has a perimeter of 40 cm. Its length is x cm.\n\n(a) Write the width in terms of x.\n(b) Show that the area is A = 20x − x².\n(c) State the value of x that maximises the area.\n(d) Calculate the maximum area.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A rectangle has a perimeter of 40 cm. Its length is x cm."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Write the width in terms of x.\n(b) Show that the area is A = 20x − x².\n(c) State the value of x that maximises the area.\n(d) Calculate the maximum area."}]}]},"e":"Width 20 − x gives A = 20x − x², and completing the square gives 100 − (x − 10)², so the maximum area is 100 cm² at x = 10: a square.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"quadratic functions","st":"quadratic functions","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":36,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"A downward parabola y = −(x − h)² + k peaks at x = h; completing the square locates it exactly.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the width when the length is x, in centimetres.","kind":"expression","points":1,"hints":["The two lengths and two widths total 40.","So one length plus one width is 20."],"strand":"D(i)","accepted":["20 - x"]},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"State the coefficient of x in the area expression 20x − x².","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["The area is length × width.","Multiply out x(20 − x)."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":20,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"State the length that maximises the area, in centimetres.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Complete the square: A = 100 − (x − 10)².","The squared term is smallest when it is zero."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":10,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cm"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Calculate the maximum area, in square centimetres.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Substitute x = 10 into 20x − x².","200 − 100."],"strand":"D(iv)","answer":100,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cm²"},{"id":"e","label":"(e)","prompt":"Describe the shape of the rectangle with the maximum area.","kind":"text","points":1,"hints":["What are its length and width at the maximum?","A rectangle with equal sides has a special name."],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["a square with sides of 10 cm"]}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for completing the square, 1 for x = 10. (d) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 100. (e) 1 mark.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for completing the square, 1 for x = 10. (d) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 100. (e) 1 mark.","response_tools":["rich_text","math","graph"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Complete the square in (c); it proves the maximum rather than assuming the midpoint."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 1 mark. (c) 2 marks: 1 for completing the square, 1 for x = 10. (d) 2 marks: 1 for substituting, 1 for 100. (e) 1 mark."},{"n":37,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A loan of $6 000 charges 1.5% interest per month on the outstanding balance. No repayments are made for 8 months.\n\n(a) State the monthly multiplier.\n(b) Calculate the amount owed after 8 months, to the nearest cent.\n(c) Calculate the total interest charged. Explain why this exceeds 8 × 1.5% of $6 000.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A loan of "},{"type":"text","text":"$6 000 charges 1.5% interest per month on the outstanding balance. No repayments are made for 8 months."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the monthly multiplier.\n(b) Calculate the amount owed after 8 months, to the nearest cent.\n(c) Calculate the total interest charged. Explain why this exceeds 8 × 1.5% of "},{"type":"text","text":"$6 000."}]}]},"e":"6000 × 1.015^8 = $6 758.94, so the interest is $758.94 against $720 for constant simple charges, because the balance compounds monthly.","m":8,"df":"hard","tp":"compound interest","st":"compound interest","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":37,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"Monthly compounding multiplies the balance repeatedly, so the total interest exceeds the sum of the simple monthly rates.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the monthly multiplier.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["A 1.5% charge adds 1.5% of the balance.","Write 101.5% as a decimal."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":1.015,"tolerance":0.0005},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the amount owed after 8 months, to the nearest cent.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use 6000 × 1.015^8.","1.015^8 ≈ 1.12649."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":6758.94,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"$"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the total interest charged.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Interest paid is the amount owed minus the money borrowed.","Subtract $6 000 from your 8-month balance."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":758.94,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"$"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why the interest exceeds 8 × 1.5% of $6 000.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["What balance is each month''s 1.5% charged on?","Does the balance stay at $6 000?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["each month''s interest is charged on a balance that already includes earlier interest, so the interest itself earns interest and the total exceeds the $720 a constant charge would give"],"keywords":[["month''s"],["interest"],["charged"],["balance"],["already"],["includes"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 1.12649, 1 for $6 758.94. (c) 1 mark. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the growing balance, 1 for the $720 comparison, 1 for the consequence.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 1.12649, 1 for $6 758.94. (c) 1 mark. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the growing balance, 1 for the $720 comparison, 1 for the consequence.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Give the exact formula in (b) and compare with $720 explicitly in (d)."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 1.12649, 1 for $6 758.94. (c) 1 mark. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the growing balance, 1 for the $720 comparison, 1 for the consequence."},{"n":38,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A fractal design is built from squares. The first square has area 64 cm². Each new square has 1/4 of the area of the previous one, and the design contains infinitely many squares.\n\n(a) State the area of the second square.\n(b) Calculate the total area of the first 3 squares.\n(c) Calculate the total area of the infinite design.\n(d) Explain why infinitely many squares occupy a finite area.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A fractal design is built from squares. The first square has area 64 cm². Each new square has 1/4 of the area of the previous one, and the design contains infinitely many squares."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) State the area of the second square.\n(b) Calculate the total area of the first 3 squares.\n(c) Calculate the total area of the infinite design.\n(d) Explain why infinitely many squares occupy a finite area."}]}]},"e":"Areas 64, 16, 4, … sum to 64/(1 − 0.25) = 85.33 cm². Because each increment is a quarter of the last, the total converges: an infinite number of squares with a finite combined area.","m":9,"df":"hard","tp":"limits of series","st":"limits of series","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":38,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"An infinite geometric series with |r| < 1 converges; the sum is a/(1 − r).","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"State the area of the second square, in square centimetres.","kind":"numeric","points":1,"hints":["Each square has a quarter of the area of the one before.","Divide 64 by 4."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":16,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cm²"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the total area of the first three squares.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The third square has area 4.","64 + 16 + 4."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":84,"tolerance":0,"unit":"cm²"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the total area of the infinite design, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use S∞ = a/(1 − r) with a = 64 and r = 0.25.","64 ÷ 0.75."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":85.33,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"cm²"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why infinitely many squares can occupy a finite area.","kind":"text","points":3,"hints":["How much does each new square add compared with the last?","Does the running total keep growing without limit?"],"strand":"C(iv)","accepted":["each new square adds only a quarter of the previous one, so the increments shrink geometrically and the running total converges to 85.33 cm² rather than growing without bound"],"keywords":[["square"],["adds"],["quarter"],["previous"],["increments"],["shrink"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the third term, 1 for 84. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 0.75, 1 for 85.33. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the shrinking increment, 1 for convergence, 1 for the finite limit.","marking_criteria":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the third term, 1 for 84. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 0.75, 1 for 85.33. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the shrinking increment, 1 for convergence, 1 for the finite limit.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Quote your running totals in (d) — the convergence is shown by the numbers, not by the word ''infinite''."},"marking":"(a) 1 mark. (b) 2 marks: 1 for the third term, 1 for 84. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the formula, 1 for 0.75, 1 for 85.33. (d) 3 marks: 1 for the shrinking increment, 1 for convergence, 1 for the finite limit."},{"n":39,"t":"assess_parts","p":"Simplify and evaluate each expression, showing the index law used.\n\n(a) 3^4 × 3^2 ÷ 3^3\n(b) (5^3)^2 ÷ 5^4\n(c) 2^−3 × 2^5","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Simplify and evaluate each expression, showing the index law used."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) 3^4 × 3^2 ÷ 3^3\n(b) (5^3)^2 ÷ 5^4\n(c) 2^−3 × 2^5"}]}]},"e":"(a) 3^(4+2−3) = 3^3 = 27. (b) 5^(6−4) = 5^2 = 25. (c) 2^(−3+5) = 2^2 = 4. The laws are: add indices when multiplying, subtract when dividing, multiply when raising a power to a power.","m":7,"df":"hard","tp":"indices","st":"indices","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":39,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"C","strand":"C(i)","next_time_hint":"Handle the indices first, then evaluate the single remaining power.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Evaluate 3^4 × 3^2 ÷ 3^3.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Multiply by adding the indices, divide by subtracting.","4 + 2 − 3 = 3, so the answer is 3^3."],"strand":"C(i)","answer":27,"tolerance":0},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Evaluate (5^3)^2 ÷ 5^4.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["A power of a power multiplies the indices: (5^3)^2 = 5^6.","Then subtract the index 4."],"strand":"C(ii)","answer":25,"tolerance":0},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Evaluate 2^−3 × 2^5.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["Add the indices: −3 + 5 = 2.","Then evaluate 2^2."],"strand":"C(iii)","answer":4,"tolerance":0},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"State the index law used to simplify 2^−3 × 2^5, in the form a^m × a^n = …","kind":"expression","points":1,"hints":["What happens to the indices when powers of the same base are multiplied?","State the law in the form a^m × a^n = …"],"strand":"C(iii)","accepted":["add the indices when multiplying powers of the same base"],"keywords":[["indices"],["multiplying"],["powers"],["base"]],"minKeywordGroups":3}],"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the combined index, 1 for 27. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 5^6, 1 for 25. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 2^2, 1 for 4. (d) 1 mark.","marking_criteria":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the combined index, 1 for 27. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 5^6, 1 for 25. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 2^2, 1 for 4. (d) 1 mark.","response_tools":["rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"text","response_guidance":"Write each law before applying it; the final values alone will not earn full marks."},"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for the combined index, 1 for 27. (b) 2 marks: 1 for 5^6, 1 for 25. (c) 2 marks: 1 for 2^2, 1 for 4. (d) 1 mark."},{"n":40,"t":"assess_parts","p":"A runner''s weekly distance increases by 10% each week. She runs 12 km in week 1.\n\n(a) Calculate the distance in week 4, to 2 decimal places.\n(b) Calculate the total distance run in the first 6 weeks.\n(c) Explain why this plan cannot continue indefinitely, using a calculation for week 30.","d":{"type":"doc","content":[{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"A runner''s weekly distance increases by 10% each week. She runs 12 km in week 1."}]},{"type":"paragraph","content":[{"type":"text","text":"(a) Calculate the distance in week 4, to 2 decimal places.\n(b) Calculate the total distance run in the first 6 weeks.\n(c) Explain why this plan cannot continue indefinitely, using a calculation for week 30."}]}]},"e":"Week 4 is 12 × 1.1³ = 15.97 km; the six-week total is 92.59 km. Week 30 would be 12 × 1.1^29 ≈ 190 km, which is not physically sustainable, so the model breaks down.","m":10,"df":"hard","tp":"modelling","st":"modelling","cfg":{"source":"myp-mock-myp5-mock","question_number":40,"paper_section":"B","myp_criterion":"D","strand":"D(i)","next_time_hint":"An exponential model may fit the early data yet fail eventually; always test it at a distant value.","assessment_style":"myp_mock","mock_exam_key":"myp5-mock","parts":[{"id":"a","label":"(a)","prompt":"Calculate the distance in week 4, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":2,"hints":["The multiplier is 1.1 and week 1 is the starting point.","12 × 1.1^3."],"strand":"D(i)","answer":15.97,"tolerance":0.02,"unit":"km"},{"id":"b","label":"(b)","prompt":"Calculate the total distance for the first 6 weeks, to 2 decimal places.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use S_n = a(r^n − 1)/(r − 1) with r = 1.1.","12(1.1^6 − 1)/0.1."],"strand":"D(ii)","answer":92.59,"tolerance":0.05,"unit":"km"},{"id":"c","label":"(c)","prompt":"Calculate the weekly distance predicted for week 30, to the nearest kilometre.","kind":"numeric","points":3,"hints":["Use 12 × 1.1^29.","1.1^29 ≈ 15.86."],"strand":"D(iii)","answer":190,"tolerance":2,"unit":"km"},{"id":"d","label":"(d)","prompt":"Explain why the plan cannot continue indefinitely.","kind":"text","points":2,"hints":["Compare 190 km per week with what a person can run.","Can growth at 10% per week be sustained?"],"strand":"D(iv)","accepted":["week 30 would demand about 190 km in a week, far beyond sustainable human training, so the exponential model must fail: real training plateaus"],"keywords":[["week"],["demand"],["beyond"],["sustainable"],["human"],["training"]],"minKeywordGroups":4}],"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for 1.1^3, 1 for 15.97. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the sum formula, 1 for 0.771561, 1 for 92.59. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 15.86, 1 for 190. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the impossible distance, 1 for the conclusion.","marking_criteria":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for 1.1^3, 1 for 15.97. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the sum formula, 1 for 0.771561, 1 for 92.59. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 15.86, 1 for 190. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the impossible distance, 1 for the conclusion.","response_tools":["graph","rich_text","math"],"response_requirement":"graph","response_guidance":"Plot the weekly distances in the workspace; the shape is the argument for (d)."},"marking":"(a) 2 marks: 1 for 1.1^3, 1 for 15.97. (b) 3 marks: 1 for the sum formula, 1 for 0.771561, 1 for 92.59. (c) 3 marks: 1 for the power, 1 for 15.86, 1 for 190. (d) 2 marks: 1 for the impossible distance, 1 for the conclusion."}]'::jsonb) as x
